NAME
       bc - a calculator language

SYNOPSIS
       bc [-l] [-q] [FILE...]

DESCRIPTION
       bc reads arithmetic, one expression per line, and prints each result.
       It works with numbers of any size. Without -l it uses whole numbers:
       set scale=N to keep N digits after the decimal point. Operators are
       + - * / % (remainder) and ^ (power); variables are lower-case names.
       Type quit (or press Ctrl+D) to leave.

       Bash's own $(( )) also does whole-number arithmetic, so bc is mostly
       used when you need decimals.

OPTIONS
       -l, --mathlib
              Load the maths library and use scale=20.

       -q, --quiet
              Do not print the welcome text.

EXAMPLES
       Divide with two decimals inside a script:

           echo "scale=2; 10/3" | bc

       Work out how many MiB 5 GiB is:

           echo "5 * 1024" | bc

SEE ALSO
       expr(1), bash(1)
